e  argument uh  differently right as a as you're  preparing those documents for for the  meetings you you tend to think a little  bit differently in a way that you have  to express your thoughts again in  paragraphs and you know in more uh  i get i would say different ways uh from  the visual um but you know that that was  a different way of doing things and i  think that's uh pretty interesting  awesome thank you juan  well no i came to amazon from the  aviation industry and even though the  planning for aviation has a lot of  similarities on how we plan the last  month the scale is very different and  the largest challenge i think i faced at  the beginning of my career at amazon was  understanding that a great scientific  approach and to solve a problem may not  be the right path forward given the  growth rate of the amazon business and  i'll give you an example you know in  routing there are numerous and thousands  of publications you know about how  people have solved the routing problem  and there are many great approaches and  and many of them you know work for a few  dozen nodes a few hundred nodes a few  thousand nodes  but when you have to solve this problem  for two hundred thousand packages you  know on a particular day in a couple of  a minutes for a given city then you  really need to think about this problem  in a different way  so you know early on  i learned that in amazon scalability  matters a lot more than optimality and  you know all the approaches that we have  to put to the table have to be able to  scale to the growth of the business  otherwise you know they become you know  in a way invisible approaches  cool so let's go with it with the next  question and what is in your opinion the  most important skill a person from latin  america most developed to be successful  in ai  and data science and darwin  can i get started  uh yeah thanks for the question  i i believe that the foundation  for a successful career and machine  learning data science